  "question": {
    "text": "If $\\alpha, \\beta$ are the roots of the equation $a x^2+b x+c=0$ then $\\lim _{x \\rightarrow \\beta} \\frac{1-\\cos \\left(a x^2+b x+c\\right)}{(x-\\beta)^2}$ is"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "$(\\alpha-\\beta)^2$",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "$\\frac{1}{2}(\\alpha-\\beta)^2$",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "$\\frac{a^2}{4}(\\alpha-\\beta)^2$",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "$\\frac{a^2}{2}(\\alpha-\\beta)^2$",
      "is_correct": true
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "D"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "Hint: $a x^2+b x+c=a(x-\\alpha)(x-\\beta)$\n$\\begin{aligned}\n& \\lim _{x \\rightarrow \\beta} \\frac{1-\\cos \\left(a x^2+b x+c\\right)}{(x-\\beta)^2} \\\\\n& =\\lim _{x \\rightarrow \\beta} \\frac{1-\\cos (a(x-\\alpha)(x-\\beta))}{(x-\\beta)^2} \\\\\n& =\\lim _{x \\rightarrow \\beta} \\frac{2 \\sin ^2\\left(\\frac{a(x-\\alpha)(x-\\beta)}{2}\\right)}{(x-\\beta)^2}=\\lim _{x \\rightarrow \\beta} \\frac{2 \\sin ^2\\left(\\frac{a(x-\\alpha)(x-\\beta}{2}\\right)}{\\frac{a^2(x-\\alpha)^2(x-\\beta)^2}{4}} \\times \\frac{a^2(x-\\alpha)^2}{4} \\\\\n& =2 \\times 1 \\times \\frac{a^2(\\beta-\\alpha)^2}{4}=\\frac{a^2(\\alpha-\\beta)^2}{2}\n\\end{aligned}$"
  }
}